NAME
    apath - build an absolute path from a command-line word (//#use apath)

SYNOPSIS
    //#use abi          (required -- SYS_GETCWD and friends)
    //#use apath        cc splices /lib/lib_apath.c

DESCRIPTION
    abspath(out, a) turns a command-line path word into an absolute path
    the BIOS FRESOLVE can use, prefixing the current directory (via
    SYS_GETCWD) when the word is relative. (The file is named lib_apath.c
    to fit P8XFS's 12-char filename field; `//#use apath` names the FILE,
    not the function.)

    abspath(out, a):
      in   a   = path word from the command line; scanning STOPS at NUL,
                 CR (13), or space (32), so a whole argv line may be
                 passed and only the first word is consumed
      out  out <- the absolute path string (NUL-terminated)
      ret  the number of chars consumed from `a`

    Unlike lib_stdin's openarg(), abspath() only BUILDS the string into a
    caller-supplied buffer -- it opens nothing. That lets a command build
    two independent paths (e.g. a src and a dst) into separate buffers
    before it opens either. Relative-path resolution matters because
    FRESOLVE / FOPENDIR start at the ROOT, not the CWD, so a bare
    relative argument must be prefixed first (see the relative-path
    gotcha in the programmer docs).

SEE ALSO
    abi, dirent, cp, mv
